<body>
  <Header />
  <main>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">Ecommerce</span>
      <h1>Shopify vs Squarespace: Best Ecommerce Builder 2026</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated July 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      Shopify and Squarespace are two of the most popular website builders that merchants compare in 2026, but they serve different primary purposes. Shopify is a dedicated ecommerce platform built for selling products at scale, while Squarespace is a design-first website builder with commerce features added on. Shopify wins on product management, checkout conversion, app ecosystem, and multi-channel selling. Squarespace wins on design aesthetics, content tools, pricing, and ease of use for non-technical creators. This comparison breaks down features, design, pricing, SEO, payments, and scalability so you can pick the right builder for your store.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-top">
      <a href="https://www.shopify.com" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Shopify Free</a>
      <a href="https://www.squarespace.com" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Squarespace Free</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Shopify</th><th>Squarespace</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Best For</td><td>Dedicated online stores, high-volume sales</td><td>Content sites with light commerce</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Starting Price</td><td>$29/mo (Basic)</td><td>$23/mo (Business), $27/mo (Commerce Basic)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Product Limit</td><td>Unlimited on all plans</td><td>Unlimited on Commerce plans</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Transaction Fees</td><td>0% on Shopify Payments, up to 2% otherwise</td><td>3% on Business, 0% on Commerce</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Themes</td><td>12 free, 100+ paid</td><td>140+ templates included</td></tr>
        <tr><td>App Store</td><td>8,000+ apps</td><td>Limited built-in integrations</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Payment Gateways</td><td>Shopify Payments + 100+</td><td>Stripe, PayPal, Apple Pay</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Abandoned Cart</td><td>Built-in recovery</td><td>Commerce plans only</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Multi-Channel</td><td>Shop, Amazon, eBay, social</td><td>Limited social selling</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Blog Engine</td><td>Basic built-in blog</td><td>Strong built-in blog</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Page Builder</td><td>Drag-and-drop theme editor</td><td>Block-based Fluid Engine editor</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inventory Management</td><td>Advanced, real-time</td><td>Basic, suitable for small catalogs</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile App</td><td>iOS, Android for store management</td><td>iOS, Android for site management</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SSL Certificate</td><td>Included, auto-renewed</td><td>Included, auto-renewed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Open Source</td><td>No, proprietary SaaS</td><td>No, proprietary SaaS</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is Shopify?</h2>
    <p>Shopify is a fully hosted ecommerce platform launched in 2006 by Tobias Lutke, Daniel Weinand, and Scott Lake. Originally built to power an online snowboard store, Shopify has grown into the most popular hosted ecommerce solution in the world, powering over 4 million stores across 175 countries. The platform is purpose-built for selling products online, which means every feature from the dashboard to the checkout is optimized for commerce. You sign up, pick a theme, add products, and your store is live, with no servers to manage and no software to install.</p>
    <p>The current pricing starts at $29 per month for the Basic plan, which includes hosting, SSL, unlimited products, two staff accounts, and access to the Shopify App Store with over 8,000 apps. Higher tiers add advanced reporting, lower transaction fees, and staff accounts. Shopify also offers Shopify Plus starting at $2,300 per month for enterprise merchants. The defining technical feature is that Shopify handles the entire commerce stack, including inventory, orders, payments, shipping, and multi-channel selling through the Shop app, Amazon, eBay, and social platforms. The tradeoff is that Shopify's content tools are basic, with a limited blog engine and page builder that lags behind Squarespace for editorial content.</p>

    <h2>What Is Squarespace?</h2>
    <p>Squarespace is a design-first website builder founded in 2003 by Anthony Casalena and headquartered in New York City. Squarespace started as a tool for creatives to build beautiful websites without coding, and it has grown into a full website platform with commerce, scheduling, email marketing, and domains built in. Unlike Shopify, which is commerce-first, Squarespace is content-first, with award-winning templates and a powerful page builder that excels at portfolios, restaurants, service businesses, and lifestyle brands.</p>
    <p>The current pricing starts at $23 per month for the Business plan, which includes ecommerce with a 3% transaction fee and a 2% payment processing fee. The Commerce Basic plan at $27 per month removes transaction fees and adds unlimited products, abandoned cart recovery, and commerce analytics. The Commerce Advanced plan at $49 per month adds advanced shipping, abandoned cart automation, and gift cards. The defining technical feature is the Fluid Engine page builder, which lets you design visually striking pages with precise layout control. The tradeoff is that Squarespace's commerce features are lighter than Shopify, with fewer payment gateways, limited multi-channel selling, and a smaller app ecosystem.</p>

    <h2>Ecommerce Features and Product Management</h2>
    <p>Ecommerce functionality is where Shopify has a decisive advantage. Shopify is built specifically for selling products, while Squarespace added commerce as a secondary feature set. Both platforms can run an online store, but the depth and polish differ significantly.</p>

    <h3>Shopify Commerce Engine</h3>
    <p>Shopify includes unlimited products on every plan, real-time inventory tracking across locations, variant support with up to 100 variants per product, and a conversion-optimized checkout that is one of the best in the industry. Shopify also includes abandoned cart recovery built into the platform, automatic tax calculation for major regions, shipping label printing, and integration with over 100 payment gateways. The Shopify admin is designed for merchants, with order management, fulfillment, returns, and customer profiles all in one dashboard. For stores doing serious volume, Shopify's commerce engine is more capable and more polished than Squarespace.</p>
    <p>The advantage compounds with the App Store. If you need subscriptions, you install Recharge. If you need product reviews, you install Judge.me. If you need advanced SEO, you install Booster. Shopify's 8,000+ apps cover virtually every commerce use case, which means the platform extends far beyond its built-in features. The tradeoff is that many apps cost money, and a typical Shopify store spends $50 to $200 per month on apps.</p>

    <h3>Squarespace Commerce Features</h3>
    <p>Squarespace includes unlimited products on Commerce plans, variant support, inventory tracking, abandoned cart recovery on Commerce Advanced, and built-in tax calculation. The checkout supports Stripe, PayPal, and Apple Pay, which covers most consumer preferences. Squarespace also includes gift cards, customer accounts, and basic analytics on Commerce plans. For a small store with under 100 products, Squarespace Commerce is sufficient and easier to manage than Shopify for non-technical users.</p>
    <p>The tradeoff is that Squarespace lacks the depth for serious commerce. There is no real multi-location inventory, no native multi-channel selling beyond basic social integrations, and the app ecosystem is small with limited extensions. For stores planning to scale beyond a few hundred products or add subscriptions, memberships, or complex shipping rules, Shopify is the better choice.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for dedicated ecommerce and product management goes to <span class="winner-tag">Shopify</span>. Best for simple small stores with limited catalogs goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squarespace</span>.</p>

    <h2>Design and Templates</h2>
    <p>Design is where Squarespace has a clear advantage. Squarespace is famous for its award-winning templates, while Shopify themes are optimized for conversion rather than editorial beauty. Both platforms produce professional-looking sites, but the design philosophy differs.</p>

    <h3>Squarespace Templates and Fluid Engine</h3>
    <p>Squarespace offers over 140 templates across categories including portfolios, restaurants, stores, blogs, and service businesses. The templates are visually striking, with sophisticated typography, generous white space, and editorial layouts that look like they were designed by a professional studio. The Fluid Engine page builder, introduced in 2022 and refined through 2026, gives you precise drag-and-drop control over layout, with the ability to place elements anywhere on a grid. For brands where design is a competitive advantage, Squarespace produces more beautiful sites with less effort.</p>
    <p>Squarespace also includes built-in design tools like image editing, color palette generation, and logo creation through its partnership with Looka. The tradeoff is that Squarespace's design freedom can work against conversion, because highly customized layouts sometimes perform worse than proven commerce templates.</p>

    <h3>Shopify Themes</h3>
    <p>Shopify offers 12 free themes and over 100 paid themes ranging from $150 to $350 one-time. The free Dawn theme is well-designed and sufficient for most small stores. All Shopify themes are mobile-responsive and optimized for fast loading and high conversion. The visual theme editor lets you customize colors, fonts, and layouts without touching code. Shopify themes are generally considered the most conversion-optimized among hosted platforms, with product pages, collection pages, and checkout flows designed to maximize sales.</p>
    <p>The tradeoff is that Shopify themes are more uniform in style. They prioritize product display and clear calls to action over editorial beauty, which means they can feel less distinctive than Squarespace templates for content-driven brands.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for design aesthetics and editorial layouts goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squarespace</span>. Best for conversion-optimized commerce themes goes to <span class="winner-tag">Shopify</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pricing and Transaction Fees</h2>
    <p>Pricing is where Squarespace has an advantage at the entry level, while Shopify offers better value for stores doing real volume. The right choice depends on your product count and sales volume.</p>

    <h3>Squarespace Pricing</h3>
    <p>Squarespace Business costs $23 per month and includes ecommerce with a 3% transaction fee on top of payment processing. This is suitable for a small store testing ecommerce, but the transaction fee adds up quickly. The Commerce Basic plan at $27 per month removes the transaction fee and adds unlimited products, making it the practical choice for any serious store. The Commerce Advanced plan at $49 per month adds abandoned cart automation, advanced shipping, and gift cards. There are no additional app fees because Squarespace has limited extensions.</p>

    <h3>Shopify Pricing</h3>
    <p>Shopify Basic costs $29 per month and includes everything needed to run a store: hosting, SSL, unlimited products, two staff accounts, and Shopify Payments with 0% transaction fees. If you use a third-party gateway, Shopify charges an additional 2% transaction fee. The Shopify plan at $79 per month adds gift cards, professional reports, and lower credit card rates. Advanced Shopify at $299 per month adds custom reports and real-time carrier shipping. The hidden cost is premium apps, with a typical store spending $50 to $200 per month on apps for email marketing, reviews, and SEO.</p>
    <p>For a store doing under $5,000 per month in revenue, Squarespace Commerce Basic at $27 is slightly cheaper than Shopify Basic at $29 once you factor in app costs. For stores doing over $10,000 per month, Shopify's commerce features, app ecosystem, and multi-channel selling deliver better value despite the higher monthly cost.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for low-cost entry and small stores goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squarespace</span>. Best for value at scale and multi-channel selling goes to <span class="winner-tag">Shopify</span>.</p>

    <h2>SEO and Content Marketing</h2>
    <p>Both platforms handle SEO basics well, but Squarespace has an edge for content-driven sites while Shopify has an edge for ecommerce SEO. The right choice depends on whether your site is content-first or commerce-first.</p>

    <h3>Squarespace Content Tools</h3>
    <p>Squarespace includes a strong built-in blog engine with scheduling, categories, tags, and drag-and-drop image placement. The blog is closer to WordPress in functionality than Shopify's basic blog. Squarespace also includes podcast hosting, email campaigns, and member areas, making it a true content platform. For SEO, Squarespace handles fast page loads, SSL, clean URLs, and editable meta tags. The Fluid Engine produces semantic HTML that search engines can crawl easily.</p>

    <h3>Shopify SEO</h3>
    <p>Shopify handles technical SEO basics well: fast page loads, automatic SSL, and built-in schema markup for products. However, Shopify forces URL structures such as /collections/ and /products/ that you cannot remove. The built-in blog is functional but basic, with limited formatting options and no advanced taxonomy. Shopify's ecommerce SEO advantage comes from product schema markup, fast checkout, and multi-channel selling that drives traffic from the Shop app, Amazon, and social platforms. For stores that rely on organic product search, Shopify is solid. For stores that rely on content marketing, Squarespace's blog is stronger.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for content marketing and blog SEO goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squarespace</span>. Best for ecommerce product SEO and multi-channel traffic goes to <span class="winner-tag">Shopify</span>.</p>

    <h2>Payments and Checkout</h2>
    <p>The checkout experience directly affects conversion rates, and this is where Shopify has a clear advantage. Shopify's checkout is purpose-built for commerce and is one of the best-converting in the industry, while Squarespace's checkout is functional but less optimized.</p>

    <h3>Shopify Checkout</h3>
    <p>Shopify's checkout is one of its biggest selling points. It is optimized for conversion, supports Shop Pay for one-click checkout, and includes accelerated checkouts like Apple Pay and Google Pay. Shopify Payments is the default gateway and charges 0% transaction fees on top of standard credit card rates. The checkout is hosted on Shopify's infrastructure, which means it loads fast and is always secure. Shopify has invested heavily in checkout optimization, including features like Shop Pay installments, dynamic checkout buttons, and AI-powered fraud analysis. For conversion, Shopify's checkout is best-in-class.</p>

    <h3>Squarespace Checkout</h3>
    <p>Squarespace supports Stripe, PayPal, and Apple Pay, which covers most consumer preferences. The checkout is functional and mobile-responsive, but it lacks the one-click checkout options and fraud analysis that Shopify includes. Squarespace also charges a 3% transaction fee on the Business plan, which makes it expensive for stores doing real volume. The Commerce plans remove this fee, making them the practical choice for any serious store. For stores where checkout conversion is critical, Shopify's optimized checkout performs better in A/B tests.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for checkout conversion and payment flexibility goes to <span class="winner-tag">Shopify</span>. Best for simple payment setup on small stores goes to <span class="winner-tag">Squarespace</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pros and Cons</h2>

    <h3>Shopify Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Purpose-built ecommerce platform with unlimited products</li>
        <li>Conversion-optimized checkout with Shop Pay</li>
        <li>8,000+ apps in the App Store</li>
        <li>Multi-channel selling through Shop, Amazon, eBay, social</li>
        <li>Real-time inventory across multiple locations</li>
        <li>Advanced abandoned cart recovery built in</li>
        <li>Handles Black Friday traffic spikes without configuration</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Shopify Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Higher monthly cost starting at $29/mo</li>
        <li>Transaction fees up to 2% if not using Shopify Payments</li>
        <li>Monthly app fees can add up quickly</li>
        <li>Basic blog engine compared to Squarespace</li>
        <li>Themes less visually distinctive than Squarespace</li>
        <li>Limited content tools for editorial sites</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Squarespace Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Award-winning templates with editorial design</li>
        <li>Fluid Engine drag-and-drop page builder</li>
        <li>Lower starting price at $23/mo</li>
        <li>Strong built-in blog and podcast hosting</li>
        <li>Email campaigns and member areas built in</li>
        <li>No additional app fees, all features included</li>
        <li>Excellent for content-first brands</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Squarespace Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>3% transaction fee on Business plan</li>
        <li>Limited payment gateways, only Stripe and PayPal</li>
        <li>Small app ecosystem with few extensions</li>
        <li>Less optimized checkout than Shopify</li>
        <li>Limited multi-channel selling options</li>
        <li>Not built for high-volume commerce</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Should Use Which?</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Dedicated Online Stores</h3>
      <p>If your primary business is selling products online and you plan to scale beyond 50 products, Shopify is the better choice. Unlimited products, advanced inventory, conversion-optimized checkout, and the App Store give you everything you need to grow. The $29 monthly fee is a reasonable price for the commerce depth, and multi-channel selling through the Shop app and Amazon drives additional revenue.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Content-First Brands with Light Commerce</h3>
      <p>If your site is primarily a portfolio, blog, restaurant, or service business with a small product component, Squarespace is the better choice. The award-winning templates, Fluid Engine page builder, and strong blog engine produce more beautiful content sites than Shopify. Adding a store with under 50 products on Squarespace Commerce Basic at $27 per month is straightforward and affordable.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Artists and Creatives</h3>
      <p>If you are a photographer, designer, musician, or artist selling prints, downloads, or services, Squarespace is the stronger choice. The templates are designed for visual portfolios, and the built-in commerce handles digital downloads, print-on-demand integrations, and service bookings. Shopify can do this but feels overbuilt for a creative selling a handful of products.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Multi-Channel Retailers</h3>
      <p>If you sell across Amazon, eBay, Google Shopping, TikTok, and Instagram alongside your own store, Shopify is the better choice. Native multi-channel integrations sync inventory and orders across channels, and the Shop app drives additional discovery. Squarespace has limited multi-channel options, making it unsuitable for serious multi-channel retail.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Subscription and Membership Businesses</h3>
      <p>If you sell subscriptions, memberships, or recurring products, Shopify is the better choice. The Recharge app integrates deeply with Shopify for subscription management, and Shopify's API supports custom membership flows. Squarespace has member areas but they are limited and less flexible for complex subscription models.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Launching a 200-product apparel store</h3>
      <p>A founder wants to launch an apparel store with 200 products, variants for size and color, and integration with Instagram Shopping. Shopify Basic at $29 per month handles unlimited products, variant management, and Instagram Shopping natively. The founder installs the Judge.me app for reviews and Klaviyo for email, spending $40 per month on apps. Squarespace Commerce Basic could handle the catalog but lacks Instagram Shopping depth and the app ecosystem, making Shopify the better choice for a serious apparel store.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Building a photographer portfolio with print sales</h3>
      <p>A photographer wants a portfolio site to showcase work and sell 30 prints. Squarespace Business at $23 per month includes a beautiful template, the Fluid Engine page builder for custom layouts, and ecommerce for print sales. The 3% transaction fee is acceptable for low volume. Shopify would handle the commerce but feels overbuilt for a 30-product portfolio, and the themes are less suited to editorial visual display. For a content-first photographer, Squarespace wins.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Scaling a coffee subscription to $50,000 per month</h3>
      <p>A coffee roaster runs a subscription business shipping beans to 1,500 subscribers monthly. Shopify Basic at $29 per month, combined with the Recharge subscription app at $60 per month, handles recurring billing, subscriber management, and fulfillment. The App Store provides integrations for inventory, shipping, and email. Squarespace has member areas but cannot handle complex subscription fulfillment at scale, making Shopify the necessary choice for a growing subscription business.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Running a restaurant site with online ordering</h3>
      <p>A restaurant needs a website with menu display, online ordering, and reservation booking. Squarespace at $23 per month includes a restaurant template, the Fluid Engine for menu layout, and integrates with OpenTable for reservations. Online ordering can be handled through Squarespace Commerce or a third-party integration. Shopify is not designed for restaurants and would require apps for menu display and reservations, making Squarespace the better choice for a restaurant site.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p><strong>Best for dedicated ecommerce: Shopify.</strong> Shopify's purpose-built commerce engine, conversion-optimized checkout, 8,000+ app ecosystem, and multi-channel selling make it the best choice for merchants whose primary business is selling products online. At $29 per month, the Basic plan is competitive and the platform scales smoothly into enterprise with Shopify Plus.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for content-first brands with light commerce: Squarespace.</strong> Squarespace's award-winning templates, Fluid Engine page builder, and strong blog engine make it the best choice for portfolios, restaurants, service businesses, and creatives who need a beautiful website that also sells a small number of products. At $23 per month, the Business plan is affordable and the Commerce plans at $27 remove transaction fees for serious stores.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for small budgets: Squarespace.</strong> For stores doing under $5,000 per month with under 50 products, Squarespace Commerce Basic at $27 per month is slightly cheaper than Shopify Basic at $29 per month once you factor in Shopify app costs. Squarespace includes most features without additional app fees.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended approach:</strong> Start on Shopify if you are launching a dedicated online store, plan to scale beyond 50 products, or need multi-channel selling. Start on Squarespace if your site is content-first with a small commerce component, you prioritize design, or you have a tight budget. Most merchants pick the right platform within the first 30 days of a free trial, and both platforms handle growth well for their respective use cases.</p>
    </div>

